INT. MERCY GENERAL HOSPITAL - NURSES' STATION - NIGHT

We see a long hallway with flickering fluorescent lights, which is a metaphor for how the main character feels inside. It is 3 AM. CLOSE ON a clock on the wall.

THE NEW GIRL, 19, sits at the station in oversized scrubs, her badge flipped backwards. She is clearly nervous and has never worked a night shift before, which we can tell because she keeps looking at her phone.

THE NEW GIRL
(straightening a stack of charts that are already straight, then knocking them over)
Okay. Okay. Breathe.

A voice crackles from the intercom.

CHARGE NURSE (V.O.)
Station, it's Bea. Are you there?

THE NEW GIRL
(grabbing the handset too hard, nearly dropping it)
Yes! Here! Station! Hi!

CHARGE NURSE (V.O.)
Room 12's call light is on. Can you check on Mr. Kessler?

THE NEW GIRL
(standing, tripping on her own lanyard)
Totally. Absolutely. Going now.

We see her walk down the hallway to Room 12, past a vending machine that hums. She stops outside the door.

KESSLER (O.S.)
Who's that? Is that Kim?

THE NEW GIRL
(sighing, opening the door a crack)
It's not Kim, Mr. Kessler. It's... it's me. The new one.

KESSLER (O.S.)
Oh. Kip said you'd be shorter.

THE NEW GIRL
(laughing, wiping her eyes with the back of her hand)
Kip is the other night nurse. He says a lot of things.

KESSLER (O.S.)
(softly)
You've got kind hands. I can hear it in your steps.

THE NEW GIRL
(pausing in the doorway, pressing a hand to her chest)
Thank you. I think that's the nicest thing anyone has said all week.

The intercom crackles again.

CHARGE NURSE (V.O.)
Priya, you still there? Your mom's on line two. She says you left your lunch in the car.

PRIYA
(burying her face in her hands, shoulders shaking with laughter)
I'm going to quit. I'm going to quit tonight.

KESSLER (O.S.)
(warmly)
You won't.

CUT TO:

INT. NURSES' STATION - LATER

Priya sits back down. For the first time all night she doesn't check her phone.
